WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.862 --> 00:00:06.677
Let's change it.

00:00:07.677 --> 00:00:08.633
And that one,

00:00:09.804 --> 00:00:10.687
take it off.

00:00:12.113 --> 00:00:15.764
Right in the heart of the Marrakech medina, a luxury riad.

00:00:18.972 --> 00:00:20.059
It's 10 o'clock in the morning.

00:00:20.772 --> 00:00:23.421
Souad, the housekeeper, doesn't have a minute to lose.

00:00:25.679 --> 00:00:27.750
Today, she has to welcome 12 people.

00:00:29.227 --> 00:00:31.757
And at 1,000 euros a night, everything has to be perfect.

00:00:43.404 --> 00:00:48.361
Three families from the Paris area rented this riad together for a week-long vacation.

00:00:56.018 --> 00:00:59.668
The charm of oriental luxury mixed with modern design,

00:01:00.178 --> 00:01:01.893
that's what makes this riad unique.

00:01:02.521 --> 00:01:03.930
And that's why it's so successful.

00:01:04.524 --> 00:01:07.410
So, gentlemen, do you want to see the massage room?

00:01:07.784 --> 00:01:09.619
The massage room? Of course we do.

00:01:13.421 --> 00:01:16.273
There's music, candles, everything for relaxation.

00:01:18.026 --> 00:01:20.257
Sir, ma'am, this is your suite.

00:01:20.478 --> 00:01:24.235
The riad has 5 bedrooms, including 2 suites like this one.

00:01:24.723 --> 00:01:27.116
All of them have a king-size bed,

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:31.434
a luxury bathroom,

00:01:32.877 --> 00:01:36.628
but most importantly, a flat-screen TV and internet access.

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:42.877
Tourists want a change of scenery, sure, but not at the expense of modern technology.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:47.524
That's what drew me in—the zen vibe, the modern touch, the color choices.

00:01:48.005 --> 00:01:53.813
It's true, it's surprising to arrive at such a trendy, beautiful riad, because it really is gorgeous.

00:01:56.001 --> 00:01:57.818
It's the surprise of opening the door.

00:01:59.804 --> 00:02:05.594
On the panoramic terrace, you discover Marrakech with a breathtaking view of the Atlas Mountains.

00:02:06.595 --> 00:02:08.446
But what justifies the price of this riad,

00:02:09.074 --> 00:02:10.042
is the service.

00:02:10.874 --> 00:02:12.589
It's worthy of a 5-star hotel.

00:02:16.103 --> 00:02:18.972
A live-in cook who prepares regional dishes,

00:02:19.533 --> 00:02:20.415
but also,

00:02:21.247 --> 00:02:25.254
a masseuse, a waiter, a gardener, a housekeeper, and a driver.

00:02:25.882 --> 00:02:29.176
A whole team of staff catering to your every need, 24/7.

00:02:32.504 --> 00:02:39.923
A dream vacation that would cost them three times as much in France. That's why Marrakech has become one of the French's favorite destinations.

00:02:43.726 --> 00:02:49.278
Less than 3 hours by plane from any European capital, Morocco welcomes 8 million tourists a year.

00:02:50.314 --> 00:02:53.998
Tourism has become the country's main source of income.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.704
But King Mohammed VI doesn't want to stop there. He plans to make Morocco Africa's top tourist destination.

00:03:05.492 --> 00:03:08.056
For the past two years, this is what the country looks like.

00:03:11.162 --> 00:03:12.181
A huge construction site.

00:03:13.115 --> 00:03:17.071
Entire cities are springing up. Workers are on the job 7 days a week.

00:03:23.794 --> 00:03:33.556
But can Morocco handle such a flood of tourists without covering its coasts in concrete, without disrupting its traditions, and above all, without losing its legendary hospitality?

00:03:38.717 --> 00:03:41.482
MOROCCO, THE NEW ELDORADO?

00:03:51.808 --> 00:03:54.694
You can't visit Marrakech without checking out the souk.

00:03:55.713 --> 00:03:57.835
A colorful world with a strange vibe.

00:04:05.305 --> 00:04:07.529
40,000 artisans sell just about everything here.

00:04:08.395 --> 00:04:13.947
As they wander, tourists feel like they're experiencing the country, understanding how Moroccans live.

00:04:19.278 --> 00:04:23.302
This afternoon, our three French women leave the riad for a shopping trip.

00:04:35.033 --> 00:04:36.595
Great, that looks good on you.

00:04:39.906 --> 00:04:40.636
How much is it?

00:04:40.942 --> 00:04:43.166
That one is 45 euros.

00:04:43.675 --> 00:04:45.087
Oh no, that's too expensive.

00:04:46.212 --> 00:04:47.970
No, ma'am, that's the price.

00:04:49.193 --> 00:04:51.202
I'll take it for 120 dirhams.

00:04:53.973 --> 00:04:55.714
150, with a nice smile.

00:04:55.806 --> 00:04:58.209
No, 120, there's the same one next door.

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:08.286
In the end, they get the price they wanted: 120 dirhams.

00:05:10.181 --> 00:05:12.434
How much is that in euros?

00:05:12.732 --> 00:05:13.710
That's 12 euros.

00:05:22.911 --> 00:05:26.290
Behind this postcard image, the reality is very different.

00:05:27.716 --> 00:05:29.295
Authenticity is just a facade now.

00:05:30.959 --> 00:05:32.894
Because the Medina no longer belongs to Moroccans.

00:05:33.675 --> 00:05:39.091
Behind the doors lining the alleys, most traditional riads have been bought by foreigners.

00:05:46.935 --> 00:05:48.955
Some have even bought entire neighborhoods.

00:05:49.584 --> 00:05:53.471
Blocks of houses turned into personal palaces of several thousand square meters.

00:05:54.439 --> 00:05:57.546
Like this Italian businessman who wanted to stay anonymous.

00:05:58.548 --> 00:06:00.159
How many houses did you buy here?

00:06:00.195 --> 00:06:00.966
A lot.

00:06:02.238 --> 00:06:03.590
You have 12, right?

00:06:04.422 --> 00:06:05.549
I have more than that.

00:06:07.862 --> 00:06:10.823
Can we take a look or you don't want to show? No, you don't want to show.

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:15.288
Thousands of square meters sold for next to nothing.

00:06:16.205 --> 00:06:18.276
That's what attracted buyers from all over the world.

00:06:19.617 --> 00:06:21.723
As a result, house prices have skyrocketed.

00:06:22.741 --> 00:06:28.769
A riad bought for 100,000 euros ten years ago can now sell, fully renovated, for up to a million euros.

00:06:29.872 --> 00:06:33.573
Astronomical sums that have ended up pushing Moroccans out of the heart of Marrakech.

00:06:40.959 --> 00:06:45.254
It's 7 p.m. and Souad, the 5-star riad's housekeeper, is leaving work.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:51.893
I've finished my workday. Now I'm starting my day as a mom.

00:06:53.013 --> 00:06:54.490
No time to waste.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:04.541
Like every evening, Souad rushes home to her two kids, who are alone at home, far from the Medina.

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:11.621
Every day, she spends an hour on the bus—her only moment of peace.

00:07:13.494 --> 00:07:14.405
It helps me relax a bit.

00:07:16.467 --> 00:07:17.937
It's the only time I have for myself.

00:07:21.332 --> 00:07:24.915
At 34, Souad isn't quite like other Moroccan women.

00:07:26.256 --> 00:07:27.122
She's divorced.

00:07:28.989 --> 00:07:32.792
It's legal, but very frowned upon in this traditional country.

00:07:33.947 --> 00:07:36.731
She lives far from her family and raises her two kids alone.

00:07:37.427 --> 00:07:42.436
She lives in El Koudia, a working-class neighborhood about ten kilometers from downtown.

00:07:43.492 --> 00:07:50.837
All day, I'm in the riad. There's a hot pool, jacuzzis, everything's beautiful, the decor is amazing.

00:07:52.792 --> 00:07:54.932
And then I come home—this is reality.

00:07:55.390 --> 00:08:00.670
The neighborhood is working-class, but it's a good reality. At home, I've got my kids: that's my luxury.

00:08:03.947 --> 00:08:07.156
The street is lined with little grocery stores, cheaper than downtown.

00:08:13.455 --> 00:08:15.441
I'm going to get some bread and eggs.

00:08:16.484 --> 00:08:17.761
Thanks, goodbye.

00:08:24.558 --> 00:08:26.578
Here, there are no streetlights in the alleys.

00:08:30.823 --> 00:08:33.505
No lights in the entryway of Souad's building either.

00:08:34.354 --> 00:08:38.259
But the rent is cheap—75 euros a month, without running water.

00:08:39.006 --> 00:08:42.147
That's the most she can afford on her 200-euro salary.

00:08:43.115 --> 00:08:44.032
Hello!

00:08:44.575 --> 00:08:48.837
She shares this tiny two-room place with Diae, 12, and Aitam, 11.

00:08:49.991 --> 00:08:53.692
They all sleep in the same room, which is also the dining and living room.

00:08:56.125 --> 00:08:58.017
You need to tidy all this up for me.

00:09:05.528 --> 00:09:07.599
What did you do today?

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:14.227
We went to school. And tonight, we cleaned the house like you asked us to.

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:21.876
A color TV and a fridge—the only two luxuries she's allowed herself.

00:09:23.692 --> 00:09:27.207
While her boys are caught up in the game, Souad keeps busy.

00:09:28.005 --> 00:09:32.453
Raising kids alone isn't easy, especially in a Muslim country like Morocco.

00:09:35.696 --> 00:09:38.073
A divorced woman is looked down on.

00:09:38.718 --> 00:09:39.719
I don't talk to anyone.

00:09:40.517 --> 00:09:42.377
I don't have many friends.

00:09:46.239 --> 00:09:47.427
I don't go out partying,

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:50.517
so I don't get harassed.

00:09:51.417 --> 00:09:53.828
I dress properly so I don't get harassed.

00:09:54.439 --> 00:09:57.478
I speak softly, I don't laugh too loud in the street.

00:09:58.344 --> 00:09:59.261
I'm careful.

00:09:59.872 --> 00:10:01.460
But sometimes I hear

00:10:02.224 --> 00:10:04.008
what people say, but it doesn't bother me.

00:10:04.609 --> 00:10:06.629
What's important to me is providing for my kids.

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:10.500
Whatever they say, whatever they do, I don't care.

00:10:11.919 --> 00:10:14.695
Do your kids cheer you up sometimes, when you're feeling down?

00:10:14.771 --> 00:10:15.496
Yes.

00:10:15.523 --> 00:10:16.858
What do the boys say to you?

00:10:17.302 --> 00:10:27.520
"Mom, when we grow up, we'll buy you a big house with everything in it. You won't have to work anymore. We love you a lot, and we know what you do for us."

00:10:33.548 --> 00:10:36.940
Let me see your hands. You're going to wash all that with soap.

00:10:38.478 --> 00:10:40.178
Stand up straight, like that.

00:10:45.408 --> 00:10:46.744
Show me your homework.

00:10:48.022 --> 00:10:49.312
Don't you want to do some exercises?

00:10:49.804 --> 00:10:54.490
It's 10 p.m., and like every night, Souad focuses on her kids' homework.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:11:02.300
She still hasn't eaten, and tomorrow she'll get up at 6 a.m. as usual to go work at the riad.

00:11:16.137 --> 00:11:18.904
In Marrakech, traditions seem hard to bear.

00:11:20.144 --> 00:11:24.286
And yet, they seem to fade away when it comes to attracting tourists.

00:11:41.434 --> 00:11:43.760
Of course, there are the traditional belly dancers.

00:11:51.061 --> 00:11:57.716
But what really attracts partygoers from all over the world are these huge nightclubs that can rival Ibiza or Miami.

00:11:59.057 --> 00:12:04.847
Techno music, alcohol, and women showing skin. Here, you quickly forget you're in a Muslim country.

00:12:15.033 --> 00:12:17.020
Vincent Benvenuti is a real estate agent.

00:12:18.174 --> 00:12:23.336
He's been living in Marrakech for 10 years and has seen the city's transformation and the price boom up close.

00:12:28.327 --> 00:12:31.926
If before, he mostly sold riads and traditional houses,

00:12:32.351 --> 00:12:37.886
now he steers his clients toward new apartments or empty lots, far from downtown. Because now,

00:12:38.344 --> 00:12:40.348
the best deals are in the countryside.

00:12:45.135 --> 00:12:47.512
Like here, 12 km from Marrakech.

00:12:47.988 --> 00:12:51.791
Vincent, the real estate agent, sold this land to a retired Frenchman.

00:12:53.590 --> 00:13:01.638
Two years ago, a former construction entrepreneur bought one hectare of empty land for 100,000 euros. And he built this luxurious home on it.

00:13:04.592 --> 00:13:06.646
A real palace for 900,000 euros.

00:13:11.315 --> 00:13:12.088
My friend Daniel.

00:13:12.351 --> 00:13:13.307
Your car is clean.

00:13:13.746 --> 00:13:18.133
See, for once I made an effort to come meet you. How are you doing?

00:13:18.183 --> 00:13:18.970
I'm good.

00:13:20.432 --> 00:13:23.455
Daniel is proud to show us around his house.

00:13:25.084 --> 00:13:26.086
How many square meters is it in total?

00:13:26.914 --> 00:13:29.772
The house? 960 square meters of living space.

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:31.396
Shall we continue?

00:13:32.232 --> 00:13:32.962
With pleasure.

00:13:34.117 --> 00:13:35.220
I really like this staircase.

00:13:36.222 --> 00:13:37.886
It's pretty monumental, pretty big.

00:13:38.514 --> 00:13:40.557
This staircase is really a great success.

00:13:42.385 --> 00:13:43.590
This is the Moroccan living room.

00:13:44.337 --> 00:13:46.884
Everything you see here, whether it's the armchairs,

00:13:47.767 --> 00:13:48.735
the coffee tables,

00:13:50.466 --> 00:13:51.315
I had it all custom made.

00:13:52.062 --> 00:13:53.838
The comfort room.

00:13:54.457 --> 00:13:56.000
It's the relaxation room.

00:13:56.239 --> 00:13:59.210
Being a good entrepreneur, Daniel didn't skimp on the marble.

00:13:59.692 --> 00:14:01.682
I think it was worth doing.

00:14:02.911 --> 00:14:04.134
There's also a sauna.

00:14:05.509 --> 00:14:06.799
It was custom built.

00:14:07.241 --> 00:14:08.121
And a hammam.

00:14:09.601 --> 00:14:13.251
Always ready to use, temperature 35-36°C.

00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:17.580
The bathroom was also custom built.

00:14:18.409 --> 00:14:25.711
We call this a bathtub, but it's almost a pool. You have to plan ahead to take a bath, because it takes 2 cubic meters of water.

00:14:30.144 --> 00:14:33.370
Among the five bedrooms in his house, Daniel has a favorite.

00:14:34.847 --> 00:14:36.303
This is the really Moroccan

00:14:37.201 --> 00:14:38.317
bedroom.

00:14:39.397 --> 00:14:41.095
Who decided on the decor?

00:14:41.298 --> 00:14:41.985
Me.

00:14:42.453 --> 00:14:43.194
All by yourself?

00:14:43.244 --> 00:14:43.920
Yes.

00:14:45.067 --> 00:14:49.329
The colors, the bed linens, everything—I picked it all myself.

00:14:50.398 --> 00:14:51.230
I'm a bit of an artist.

00:14:51.638 --> 00:14:52.419
Ah, that's it.

00:14:55.339 --> 00:14:56.867
This is the aperitif room.

00:14:57.376 --> 00:14:59.321
That's your favorite spot, huh?

00:15:02.071 --> 00:15:04.847
Then there's the pool here, with the fountain pool.

00:15:07.037 --> 00:15:09.006
How much time do you plan to spend here each year?

00:15:09.668 --> 00:15:10.670
183 days.

00:15:12.616 --> 00:15:17.988
183 days a year is the minimum you have to spend abroad to avoid French taxes.

00:15:24.405 --> 00:15:28.039
By living in Morocco, Daniel pays ten times less tax than in France.

00:15:28.514 --> 00:15:30.976
He saves nearly 200,000 euros a year that way.

00:15:31.825 --> 00:15:36.867
Enough to afford household staff, since Morocco offers especially cheap labor.

00:15:38.684 --> 00:15:40.857
A big house like this, you need people.

00:15:41.468 --> 00:15:42.962
You need two people for the garden.

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:49.244
A guy for the pools and outdoor cleaning, two maids who cook.

00:15:49.821 --> 00:15:50.806
That's five people.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:55.550
Since salaries in Morocco have fewer social charges,

00:15:56.120 --> 00:16:01.838
in France I couldn't even afford a registered housekeeper. Right?

00:16:01.916 --> 00:16:02.739
Yeah, pretty much.

00:16:04.049 --> 00:16:08.446
But to build luxury homes like this, you need land—more and more land.

00:16:11.095 --> 00:16:14.660
That's why the king is giving lots of land to foreign developers.

00:16:17.784 --> 00:16:22.843
To attract high-end clients, about fifteen luxury resorts are under construction.

00:16:25.135 --> 00:16:33.183
Funded by the biggest names in international tourism, they'll have 5-star hotels, spas, and above all, spectacular golf courses.

00:16:37.478 --> 00:16:39.295
Right now, the city already has four.

00:16:40.331 --> 00:16:45.492
But about ten more are planned, which would make Marrakech the best-equipped city in Africa.

00:16:46.205 --> 00:16:48.089
A crazy offering in the middle of the desert.

00:16:52.996 --> 00:16:56.188
Thousands of cubic meters of water wasted to maintain the golf courses.

00:16:57.444 --> 00:17:00.857
Just for the pleasure of golfers who like to play a different course every day.

00:17:04.405 --> 00:17:09.702
That means less land for Moroccans, who are forced to move farther and farther from Marrakech.

00:17:20.942 --> 00:17:23.947
So here, this is the edge of the city,

00:17:24.456 --> 00:17:26.375
as it was just a year ago.

00:17:26.884 --> 00:17:29.685
So you can clearly see the boundary of the urban area.

00:17:30.449 --> 00:17:36.680
A hundred meters away, right across from Marrakech's former edge, social housing is going up at full speed.

00:17:38.548 --> 00:17:41.315
So basically, people are being pushed farther and farther out.

00:17:41.315 --> 00:17:48.258
Exactly. Like in all big cities, downtown becomes offices, luxury buildings, shops, and much more expensive.

00:17:48.766 --> 00:17:54.575
And you have to go to the northern outskirts, like in most cities, it's usually the north that's the most working-class.

00:17:56.188 --> 00:17:57.699
Such a housing shortage,

00:17:58.921 --> 00:18:03.896
that the government keeps launching social housing projects for miles, all the way to the new city of Tamansourt.

00:18:14.405 --> 00:18:19.906
Located 20 kilometers from Marrakech, this sprawling city will welcome the new suburbanites.

00:18:22.402 --> 00:18:25.186
Construction as far as the eye can see, over 1,200 hectares.

00:18:27.376 --> 00:18:33.522
Eventually, Tamansourt should house 300,000 people—a population equal to the city of Nantes.

00:18:40.263 --> 00:18:44.524
For now, sheep and cows still have a patch of green, but not for much longer.

00:18:49.736 --> 00:18:53.692
These big complexes are reminiscent of the ghetto projects built in France in the 1960s.

00:19:00.314 --> 00:19:01.417
Same smooth facades,

00:19:02.317 --> 00:19:03.794
same tiny windows,

00:19:04.490 --> 00:19:05.594
same endless hallways.

00:19:07.444 --> 00:19:12.453
And despite the sun and the pink paint, it still feels like a dormitory town.

00:19:20.634 --> 00:19:25.441
Already, over 120,000 homes have been taken, have been bought.

00:19:26.144 --> 00:19:32.826
So right now, we're in the delivery phase, where everything's being handed over. That's why it feels a bit like a ghost town,

00:19:33.438 --> 00:19:37.275
with no stores yet, very few homes, very few people.

00:19:37.716 --> 00:19:41.366
So everything's getting set up. It's a bit surprising, because it really feels like a city

00:19:41.825 --> 00:19:43.438
that came out of nowhere, out of nothing.

00:19:56.035 --> 00:20:01.876
Fatima just bought a 46-square-meter apartment in Tamansourt, in one of its new towers, for 20,000 euros.

00:20:03.438 --> 00:20:05.865
She lives here with her husband and two grown kids.

00:20:08.769 --> 00:20:10.008
Here's our bedroom.

00:20:12.147 --> 00:20:13.913
And here, the kids' room.

00:20:14.592 --> 00:20:18.565
Compared to the riad where she lived in the Medina, her apartment seems tiny.

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:22.792
But for Fatima, it marks a deep cultural shift in her life.

00:20:23.743 --> 00:20:27.020
Until recently, like many Moroccans, she lived with her extended family.

00:20:27.614 --> 00:20:31.010
There were 15 of them sharing the same house, all generations together.

00:20:35.764 --> 00:20:36.833
There's nowhere better than here.

00:20:38.982 --> 00:20:44.032
Before, we were all mixed together with my brothers, sisters, and their kids.

00:20:45.696 --> 00:20:48.174
Now, I'm independent with my own family.

00:20:49.601 --> 00:20:52.249
When it's just you and your kids, you live in peace.

00:20:54.983 --> 00:20:57.784
For her, freedom and modern comfort are priceless.

00:20:58.650 --> 00:21:02.572
Fatima and her husband took out a 20-year loan to live in Tamansourt.

00:21:08.938 --> 00:21:11.893
500 kilometers further north is the city of Fez.

00:21:13.370 --> 00:21:17.139
This former imperial city refuses to suffer Marrakech's fate.

00:21:18.378 --> 00:21:21.943
It's not ready to sacrifice its authenticity for mass tourism.

00:21:23.777 --> 00:21:26.171
In Fez, time seems to have stood still.

00:21:26.663 --> 00:21:31.842
Traditional customs are still very much alive here, and medieval architecture dominates.

00:21:41.655 --> 00:21:47.971
But above all, Morocco's cultural capital is home to the world's largest medina, a UNESCO World Heritage site.

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:55.628
Hundreds of alleyways form a labyrinth where it's a pleasure to get lost.

00:21:58.972 --> 00:22:03.387
A charming bazaar, with the only landmarks being the medina's 350 mosques.

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:10.398
Authentic Fez is winning over more and more tourists.

00:22:12.606 --> 00:22:15.882
Nearly 3 million people visit the famous tanneries every year.

00:22:21.078 --> 00:22:23.387
In the colorful pits with their medieval charm,

00:22:23.913 --> 00:22:25.831
men turn hides into leather.

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:32.419
These age-old techniques are passed down from generation to generation.

00:22:33.777 --> 00:22:35.016
It's a mesmerizing sight.

00:22:39.940 --> 00:22:45.000
An exceptional heritage that the residents of Fez's medina have decided to preserve.

00:22:46.494 --> 00:22:50.127
Unlike Marrakech, their ancestral homes are not for sale.

00:22:52.877 --> 00:22:54.694
It's thanks to the efforts of this young woman.

00:22:55.984 --> 00:23:00.365
With help from the region, she managed to bring together 30 families for a project.

00:23:00.959 --> 00:23:04.898
Turning their riads into guesthouses instead of selling them to foreigners.

00:23:10.144 --> 00:23:14.439
Originally from Fez, Layla Skalli, 32, is an architect.

00:23:15.509 --> 00:23:20.263
But for the past two years, she's been training Moroccan families to host foreign tourists.

00:23:22.606 --> 00:23:24.983
She taught them the rules of Western living.

00:23:25.984 --> 00:23:29.023
Food, comfort, hygiene.

00:23:33.421 --> 00:23:36.256
This morning marks the end of their training.

00:23:36.782 --> 00:23:39.634
She's going to hand out the same linens to everyone.

00:23:42.724 --> 00:23:45.475
These are the towels we prepared with the logo,

00:23:45.882 --> 00:23:47.173
Ziyarates Fès,

00:23:48.548 --> 00:23:50.976
so here you go, I'll show you all.

00:23:54.388 --> 00:23:55.918
It's written in French and Arabic.

00:23:59.287 --> 00:24:02.148
We'll get our name out there with this Ziyarates Fès logo.

00:24:02.453 --> 00:24:04.949
In Arabic, "ziyarates" means "visit."

00:24:08.667 --> 00:24:10.670
Each house will have a full set of linens.

00:24:11.095 --> 00:24:12.079
Bath towels,

00:24:12.538 --> 00:24:21.213
hand towels, and sheets. It's essential for tourists, because traditionally, in riads, you just get a blanket to sleep with.

00:24:26.086 --> 00:24:31.400
5092 dirhams, or 460 euros. Each family has to pay for their set.

00:24:32.079 --> 00:24:37.157
They didn't pay up front. We paid for them. And they'll pay us back in a year.

00:24:37.563 --> 00:24:39.210
All of this, of course, is interest-free.

00:24:39.668 --> 00:24:42.911
But those aren't the only benefits these modest families get.

00:24:43.811 --> 00:24:50.042
Layla convinced the local authorities to help them financially, like paying for bathroom renovations, for example.

00:24:52.368 --> 00:24:56.969
With her team, she hopes to develop more responsible tourism in Fez.

00:24:59.397 --> 00:25:08.639
At some point, this tourist flow also needs to benefit Moroccans, especially those who live here, so the city keeps belonging to them.

00:25:08.934 --> 00:25:16.850
90% of these families used to sell their homes, but now, instead of selling, they keep them and stay.

00:25:17.495 --> 00:25:20.398
Thanks to the association, Naïma was able to save her riad.

00:25:20.993 --> 00:25:25.305
With her husband and daughter, today she's hosting a couple from Corrèze.

00:25:27.130 --> 00:25:28.165
Hello, nice to meet you.

00:25:30.008 --> 00:25:33.794
For these four days of relaxation in Fez, they chose to stay with locals.

00:25:40.365 --> 00:25:43.929
At 25 euros a night, it's an affordable way to experience Moroccan life.

00:25:45.882 --> 00:25:49.439
These stairs are tall, they're stairs for giants.

00:25:54.049 --> 00:25:59.363
Unlike Marrakech, here the riads are renovated in the purest Moroccan tradition.

00:25:59.923 --> 00:26:02.894
No high-tech gadgets, and definitely no designer furniture.

00:26:03.419 --> 00:26:05.713
It's really pretty, it's beautiful.

00:26:06.561 --> 00:26:09.061
And it's way friendlier than a hotel.

00:26:10.857 --> 00:26:12.266
Look at Marrakech now,

00:26:12.707 --> 00:26:17.207
All the riads, all the houses, they're sold, that's it, to foreigners.

00:26:18.005 --> 00:26:24.643
So here in Fez, we don't want to make the same mistake, so we did this to keep the houses.

00:26:24.881 --> 00:26:26.273
That's a shame, I wanted to buy it from you.

00:26:30.738 --> 00:26:37.886
Everyone wins. The family keeps their house and the tourists travel with a purpose. That's called solidarity tourism.

00:26:39.227 --> 00:26:42.011
Here, there's a different culture, different people,

00:26:42.691 --> 00:26:46.307
with different customs, who can teach us things.

00:26:47.156 --> 00:26:49.752
And those are the things we should bring home with us.

00:27:13.217 --> 00:27:17.105
More and more vacationers are fleeing the cities, looking for wide open spaces.

00:27:18.056 --> 00:27:21.383
Of course, there are the classic camel rides in the heart of the desert.

00:27:23.336 --> 00:27:25.611
But some tourists want to travel differently.

00:27:26.052 --> 00:27:30.059
They want their visit to benefit the region, not just the big tour operators.

00:27:38.123 --> 00:27:43.709
Six hours from Marrakech, Anergui is a deep, remote valley in the heart of the Atlas Mountains.

00:27:45.271 --> 00:27:50.059
The Julien family came from Toulon, in southern France, to spend a week on vacation here.

00:27:50.687 --> 00:27:55.814
Laurence and Jean-Luc brought their daughters along for the adventure: Margot, 12, and Émilie, 4.

00:27:57.122 --> 00:27:59.567
Their guide, Lassène, is a local guy.

00:28:00.135 --> 00:28:01.584
Hi, how are you?

00:28:01.945 --> 00:28:03.403
Is there enough water right now?

00:28:06.258 --> 00:28:10.840
His goal? To develop tourism in the valley, one of the poorest in the region.

00:28:11.179 --> 00:28:17.699
For the valley, it's the only way to develop and improve people's standard of living.

00:28:21.315 --> 00:28:24.252
And off they go for a five-hour hike under the blazing sun.

00:28:25.373 --> 00:28:28.718
Luckily, the mules carry the bags—and even the youngest daughter.

00:28:38.633 --> 00:28:41.095
It's a steep climb, but the scenery is breathtaking.

00:28:54.269 --> 00:28:57.071
Around the bends, you find fortress-like villages.

00:28:57.665 --> 00:29:00.653
This is where the Berber tribes live. Former nomads.

00:29:10.585 --> 00:29:14.439
These landscapes of snow, greenery, flowers,

00:29:15.135 --> 00:29:17.555
and little adobe villages—it's really beautiful.

00:29:18.022 --> 00:29:21.638
Their week of total escape costs them 1,760 euros, all included.

00:29:22.996 --> 00:29:25.984
That's enough to support five or six families in the valley.

00:29:47.412 --> 00:29:48.789
The kids are in heaven.

00:29:50.109 --> 00:29:54.106
They're happy here, it's really nice. It's paradise. Kind of, yeah.

00:29:56.867 --> 00:30:01.672
Today, the Julien family is about to experience a special moment—a unique ceremony.

00:30:02.555 --> 00:30:04.473
In the valley, water is precious.

00:30:05.305 --> 00:30:07.665
One spring usually supplies several villages.

00:30:08.395 --> 00:30:16.358
To avoid disputes among Berber farmers, they have to elect someone to distribute the water as fairly as possible.

00:30:16.884 --> 00:30:22.317
Today is the day when the tribe gathers here, at the spring,

00:30:22.775 --> 00:30:28.752
because it's from this spring that they irrigate all the fields and little plots in the valley.

00:30:29.346 --> 00:30:35.050
So they meet here to choose the man, as they call him—the "water man."

00:30:36.033 --> 00:30:42.657
Today, the election is expected to be heated. After two years of loyal service, the water chief refuses another term.

00:30:45.118 --> 00:30:49.804
Leave me alone, I don't want to keep going, I can't take managing the water anymore.

00:30:52.589 --> 00:30:56.782
Come on, make an effort, trust us, we'll help you, we'll give you a hand.

00:30:59.714 --> 00:31:00.925
No, I don't want to anymore.

00:31:01.672 --> 00:31:11.977
I tried to be fair, I tried to do right by everyone. I was impartial and it only brought me trouble. I became the enemy of the people. Why? For nothing.

00:31:12.945 --> 00:31:14.677
It's a bit of folklore that amuses the tourists.

00:31:15.933 --> 00:31:19.346
I think he was re-elected, but I don't know if he's happy about it.

00:31:20.490 --> 00:31:22.035
I don't get it.

00:31:25.373 --> 00:31:31.298
Meanwhile, Laurence has joined the women and children of the tribe, who stay apart as Berber tradition dictates.

00:31:32.860 --> 00:31:34.728
She's trying to communicate in her own way.

00:31:35.322 --> 00:31:37.156
How old are you? 10 years old.

00:31:45.448 --> 00:31:47.179
This is Émilie, she's 4.

00:31:49.125 --> 00:31:55.213
Here, you can see it's a ceremony where they're passing things down, and it makes us wonder what we pass on to our own kids.

00:31:55.699 --> 00:32:00.993
Can a 10- or 12-year-old even imagine living without electricity, without the Internet?

00:32:01.833 --> 00:32:06.942
So I think it's important to appreciate what we have, to really value things.

00:32:17.469 --> 00:32:19.823
She's beautiful, may God protect her.

00:32:20.127 --> 00:32:24.589
After two hours of heated debate, the water chief finally gave in to the crowd's pressure.

00:32:25.077 --> 00:32:30.144
He receives the symbolic blade of grass, making him the valley's new water manager for a year.

00:32:44.286 --> 00:32:51.196
The Julien family still has four days of vacation left to discover more wonders in the valley and live at the pace of the Berber people.

00:32:52.198 --> 00:32:53.030
Far from the concrete.

00:33:03.064 --> 00:33:05.237
Rural tourism like this is still rare.

00:33:07.716 --> 00:33:12.334
To attract more visitors, Morocco is counting on its rich coastline.

00:33:17.427 --> 00:33:21.468
Thousands of kilometers of deserted beaches that the king has decided to develop.

00:33:22.572 --> 00:33:28.429
Mohammed VI has set an ambitious goal: attract 10 million tourists by 2010.

00:33:31.230 --> 00:33:36.307
So he launched a massive plan: to build six seaside resorts from scratch.

00:33:43.336 --> 00:33:51.044
The first to be built, Saïdia, is in northeastern Morocco, in a long-neglected region near the Algerian border.

00:33:51.774 --> 00:33:55.899
Land that the Moroccan state sold to a Spanish developer for next to nothing.

00:33:56.986 --> 00:33:58.497
Here's what their project looks like.

00:34:01.502 --> 00:34:03.319
The promotional film is eye-opening.

00:34:04.032 --> 00:34:10.331
Beach bars every 300 meters, residences, villas, and apartments by giant pools.

00:34:11.027 --> 00:34:16.256
A luxury mall, a private clinic, three golf courses, and above all, a marina.

00:34:20.840 --> 00:34:24.575
All in all, a real city ready to welcome 30,000 tourists.

00:34:25.169 --> 00:34:28.955
Officially, this massive project is supposed to be ready for this summer.

00:34:37.444 --> 00:34:40.382
Houses rising from the sand, skeletons of buildings.

00:34:40.823 --> 00:34:44.235
Saïdia is still under construction just weeks before the opening.

00:34:45.169 --> 00:34:46.697
It looks like a ghost town.

00:34:57.716 --> 00:35:00.942
Two years ago, we met with the project managers.

00:35:02.283 --> 00:35:06.188
Back then, optimism was the rule for the Moroccan architect,

00:35:07.139 --> 00:35:09.499
and the site director, Juan Cano.

00:35:11.434 --> 00:35:13.302
Saïdia was supposed to be a goldmine.

00:35:17.954 --> 00:35:26.477
For Saïdia, the investment we're making for the golf courses, the marina, everything, is 600 million euros.

00:35:27.478 --> 00:35:30.789
You're investing 600 million euros—how much do you hope to make?

00:35:34.788 --> 00:35:39.466
Normally, with 20–25%, we're already happy.

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:44.949
But two years later, things have changed a lot.

00:35:46.171 --> 00:35:49.719
After the real estate crisis, the Spanish developer went bankrupt.

00:35:50.398 --> 00:35:53.539
The Saïdia project now belongs to a Moroccan group.

00:35:54.185 --> 00:35:55.560
So Juan Cano is out.

00:35:57.971 --> 00:36:01.723
Enter Hassan Zerma, the new group director for the past six months.

00:36:03.081 --> 00:36:05.169
The man has changed, but not the message.

00:36:06.443 --> 00:36:10.670
Fadesa Maroc Group is doing well, our projects aren't on hold, we're still investing,

00:36:11.162 --> 00:36:14.762
on this site and others in Marrakech, Tetouan, etc.

00:36:15.748 --> 00:36:22.714
So, we see the future optimistically. God willing, we'll be ready to open the resort at the start of June.

00:36:25.135 --> 00:36:28.803
God willing, the site will be ready for the official opening in a month.

00:36:30.314 --> 00:36:35.848
But let's be honest, there's still a lot of work to do. As of now, only 2 out of 10 neighborhoods are finished.

00:36:36.748 --> 00:36:40.348
Most of the villas, buildings, and gardens are still under construction.

00:36:43.692 --> 00:36:46.629
In reality, only one out of three golf courses is ready.

00:36:51.893 --> 00:36:54.269
And out of the nine planned beachfront hotels,

00:36:54.932 --> 00:36:56.629
only two are about to open.

00:36:57.716 --> 00:37:01.027
The most majestic, the Barceló, is Saïdia's pride.

00:37:01.825 --> 00:37:04.762
A five-star hotel with Andalusian-inspired architecture.

00:37:05.475 --> 00:37:08.514
With 1,200 rooms, it's the biggest hotel in Africa.

00:37:09.363 --> 00:37:12.079
To get the palace ready in time, everyone's pitching in.

00:37:12.538 --> 00:37:19.227
Dozens of workers are busy sanding wood, landscaping gardens, and polishing every inch of the pool.

00:37:24.592 --> 00:37:30.042
Right now, it's hard to imagine what this suite will look like. The furniture is still wrapped in plastic.

00:37:31.604 --> 00:37:36.646
And for the privilege of a sea view, you'll have to pay 800 euros for a week.

00:37:38.259 --> 00:37:39.651
But according to our sources,

00:37:40.246 --> 00:37:42.113
this summer, the hotel is far from full.

00:37:44.558 --> 00:37:48.327
The marina was also supposed to be finished, but there was a little problem.

00:37:49.176 --> 00:37:50.398
Because of no prior studies,

00:37:50.840 --> 00:37:53.183
the breakwater was built—but backwards.

00:37:53.862 --> 00:37:56.324
As a result, the marina is filled with sand.

00:37:56.816 --> 00:37:58.344
Boats can't sail anymore.

00:38:03.251 --> 00:38:05.764
So for the past year, the port's been buzzing.

00:38:06.256 --> 00:38:07.631
Construction goes on day and night.

00:38:08.837 --> 00:38:11.723
They have to rebuild a new breakwater in record time.

00:38:15.305 --> 00:38:17.954
Because the marina is Saïdia's main attraction.

00:38:20.687 --> 00:38:22.079
The city is far from finished.

00:38:23.675 --> 00:38:31.230
But the promise of a spot for their boat and year-round sunshine convinced a French retired couple to buy a duplex in this building.

00:38:34.575 --> 00:38:35.747
So this is the entrance.

00:38:38.467 --> 00:38:40.127
It's very spacious and simple.

00:38:40.619 --> 00:38:43.505
Here's the dining area. And that's the living room,

00:38:44.320 --> 00:38:45.780
which opens onto the terrace.

00:38:46.697 --> 00:38:48.900
It's nice for eating meals.

00:38:49.302 --> 00:38:51.179
The apartment has three terraces like this one.

00:38:51.757 --> 00:38:53.285
So here you're facing the sea.

00:38:54.235 --> 00:38:57.173
Facing the sea, sure, but you still need a good view.

00:38:57.529 --> 00:38:59.855
You say you have a sea view, but it's pretty far away.

00:39:01.129 --> 00:39:06.528
No, I don't have a sea view. But there weren't any apartments available right on the seafront.

00:39:07.342 --> 00:39:12.062
And on top of that, a small two-story building is going up right in front of her place.

00:39:12.724 --> 00:39:14.575
The downsides are made up for by the price.

00:39:15.000 --> 00:39:20.517
This 220-square-meter duplex cost them 180,000 euros. That's three times cheaper than in France.

00:39:21.044 --> 00:39:26.061
So, this is the view of the middle of the complex and the pool. No water yet.

00:39:27.241 --> 00:39:30.704
Their building is in one of the most finished neighborhoods in Saïdia.

00:39:33.319 --> 00:39:38.276
All the units have been sold, but in April, the shutters are still stubbornly closed.

00:39:43.166 --> 00:39:46.069
You don't have many neighbors, do you?

00:39:46.884 --> 00:39:54.524
Yeah, it's really quiet, but since we're pretty solitary people, it doesn't bother us much. The only thing,

00:39:55.580 --> 00:39:58.887
if you can call it a problem, is having to go pick up the mail at the post office,

00:39:59.380 --> 00:40:03.947
because there aren't any mail deliveries yet—there are so few of us, there's no mailman coming yet.

00:40:04.915 --> 00:40:07.937
Other than that, it's just fine for me.

00:40:09.346 --> 00:40:12.724
Paul is one of the lucky few whose home was delivered on time.

00:40:13.455 --> 00:40:16.969
Because Saïdia, like the rest of the world, is feeling the financial crisis.

00:40:20.246 --> 00:40:23.709
A British developer was supposed to build part of the city.

00:40:25.933 --> 00:40:28.039
But now he can't afford to finish the work.

00:40:29.363 --> 00:40:32.419
Out of 11 construction sites, only 2 are nearing completion.

00:40:37.122 --> 00:40:39.702
All those dream homes, shown in the brochures,

00:40:40.178 --> 00:40:41.078
are still just a dream.

00:40:45.628 --> 00:40:47.648
But officially, there's no cause for concern.

00:40:48.446 --> 00:40:50.602
The resort will open in a month.

00:40:52.792 --> 00:41:01.910
To prove it, the sales director, Zouhir Yousfi, insists on taking us to visit one of the resort's highlights: the shopping center called La Médina.

00:41:03.777 --> 00:41:08.039
In less than a month, it's supposed to be inaugurated by King Mohammed VI himself.

00:41:15.033 --> 00:41:16.154
How many shops will there be?

00:41:17.275 --> 00:41:19.074
There will be about a hundred shops,

00:41:19.584 --> 00:41:21.315
ready-to-wear stores,

00:41:22.058 --> 00:41:24.557
restaurants, artisan shops,

00:41:25.135 --> 00:41:25.940
and a bit of everything.

00:41:26.629 --> 00:41:28.769
Can you name a few brands that will be here?

00:41:29.601 --> 00:41:31.808
You see here, for example, there will be Marionnaud.

00:41:32.215 --> 00:41:33.251
There will be Zara too.

00:41:33.845 --> 00:41:36.714
I don't know, maybe we'll have Lacoste. There will be Lacoste.

00:41:37.933 --> 00:41:42.117
So, there will be a lot of high-end shops.

00:41:43.064 --> 00:41:46.389
The director seems a bit uncomfortable, because reality is different.

00:41:46.964 --> 00:41:53.743
While brands like Lacoste are still planning to open a store this summer, Zara and Marionnaud have pulled out of the project.

00:41:54.185 --> 00:41:58.667
According to our information, only 20 stores remain out of the 100 originally planned.

00:41:58.803 --> 00:42:00.687
And do you think it will be finished in June?

00:42:01.213 --> 00:42:05.424
Normally, yes. It'll be ready in June. Otherwise, we'll have problems.

00:42:06.171 --> 00:42:07.563
But there are already problems.

00:42:08.276 --> 00:42:12.266
At the other end of La Médina, the door leading to the beach is cracked all over.

00:42:13.641 --> 00:42:17.071
That's something Zouhir Yousfi would have preferred we didn't notice.

00:42:20.178 --> 00:42:22.758
Excuse me, can you explain what all these cracks are?

00:42:24.405 --> 00:42:27.122
Why are there cracks everywhere? It was just built, it's weird.

00:42:27.359 --> 00:42:31.285
It was just built, but since it's a big building, so...

00:42:34.507 --> 00:42:37.954
Sorry, but I can explain it to you, but in my own way.

00:42:39.491 --> 00:42:41.388
Go ahead. No problem.

00:42:41.519 --> 00:42:51.535
Like everyone says here, a big building like this, once it's built and set on the ground, can naturally have cracks. It's normal.

00:42:51.901 --> 00:42:58.039
So right now, we're fixing it, and this part is part of the second phase, which will open in summer 2010.

00:42:58.582 --> 00:43:01.519
Buildings sinking into the sand, but that's not all.

00:43:01.994 --> 00:43:04.915
On the seafront, some hotels already look run-down.

00:43:05.645 --> 00:43:10.093
Before they've even had their first guest, they're losing tiles and parts of their plaster.

00:43:13.030 --> 00:43:15.984
Even stranger, entire neighborhoods are underwater.

00:43:16.511 --> 00:43:20.042
Because this winter, it rained in Morocco like it hasn't in 50 years.

00:43:20.704 --> 00:43:24.507
Saïdia's drainage system wasn't built for this kind of extreme weather.

00:43:25.033 --> 00:43:27.427
So they're overflowing for miles around.

00:43:35.288 --> 00:43:39.023
The dry, arid landscape has turned into a rice field.

00:43:39.533 --> 00:43:42.589
And the nearby homes were flooded for three months.

00:43:43.777 --> 00:43:48.140
Local authorities hastily dug this trench to drain the water into the sea.

00:43:50.483 --> 00:43:53.726
For Mohamed Benata, an environmental activist in the region,

00:43:54.168 --> 00:43:57.478
it's just one more absurdity in this beach resort project.

00:44:00.500 --> 00:44:01.587
That's not normal.

00:44:02.351 --> 00:44:12.657
Someone managing projects, managing development, should think, should consult scientists, consult technicians. There are serious consulting firms for this.

00:44:13.098 --> 00:44:21.417
Unfortunately, I think it's hasty decisions that caused all this mess on the Saïdia coast.

00:44:23.777 --> 00:44:27.886
But the worst thing for Mohamed is that the Saïdia resort ruined the beach.

00:44:28.463 --> 00:44:30.704
The beach he grew up with as a child.

00:44:30.976 --> 00:44:36.799
That's how it used to be. For 14 kilometers, there was a beautiful beach. We called it the "blue pearl."

00:44:37.393 --> 00:44:41.230
Unfortunately, everything was sacrificed for mass tourism.

00:44:43.438 --> 00:44:48.633
To build this large-scale tourist project, the developers leveled the dunes and their vegetation.

00:44:49.363 --> 00:44:53.200
Especially this plant, which actually had a very specific purpose.

00:44:54.422 --> 00:44:55.865
This plant is amazing.

00:44:56.324 --> 00:45:00.076
It's Ammophila arenaria. It's the one that holds the front line,

00:45:00.534 --> 00:45:10.585
on the coast. And you can see, even with the wind today, the sand stays in place. It barely moves because it's held by this wonderful plant.

00:45:13.234 --> 00:45:19.057
The difference between the old Saïdia beach and the new one is really striking.

00:45:21.292 --> 00:45:22.177
That's good!

00:45:26.901 --> 00:45:35.577
You can see the direction of the wind and the sand moving. It's no longer held by the natural vegetation that used to be there. That's sand lost for the beach.

00:45:36.765 --> 00:45:40.721
That sand will end up on the boardwalk, and then it'll go even farther.

00:45:41.553 --> 00:45:43.387
See, the sand goes really far.

00:45:44.235 --> 00:45:52.181
And now, the residents are suffering from the sand invasion. For over three years, they've had sandstorms. Every time, they have to clean their homes.

00:45:52.640 --> 00:45:58.887
But even though environmentalists keep filing complaints with the builders, they don't have much influence with Saïdia's leaders.

00:45:59.448 --> 00:46:04.830
Environmentalists say it's a disaster because before, there were plants that kept the sand on the beach,

00:46:05.475 --> 00:46:07.971
and by removing them, now there's sand all over the city.

00:46:09.125 --> 00:46:10.161
Do you have a response to that?

00:46:12.130 --> 00:46:18.303
We have to develop the seafront to welcome tourists and the residents of the villas and apartments we've sold.

00:46:19.142 --> 00:46:24.745
What do you say to the locals who say that now, in Saïdia, sand blows into the city, when before it didn't?

00:46:29.550 --> 00:46:34.932
No answer to the environmental question. Zouhir Yousfi prefers to highlight the project's benefits for the region.

00:46:35.747 --> 00:46:45.356
Once the site is finished, it will create 48,000 jobs: 8,000 direct jobs and 40,000 indirect ones. That's a plus for Saïdia and the region.

00:46:46.544 --> 00:46:51.061
The sales director does his best to hide his discomfort. But his troubles aren't over yet.

00:46:51.943 --> 00:46:54.864
Because in Saïdia, the most serious flaws are well hidden.

00:46:55.764 --> 00:47:01.672
As the city gets ready to welcome 30,000 people, we wanted to know how the wastewater will be handled.

00:47:01.893 --> 00:47:03.013
Is there a sewage treatment plant here?

00:47:03.777 --> 00:47:08.310
Yes, normally, we have a treatment plant built by the National Office of Drinking Water.

00:47:08.446 --> 00:47:10.093
And where is this plant?

00:47:10.983 --> 00:47:12.493
I'm not allowed to...

00:47:12.674 --> 00:47:13.981
I just want to see where it is.

00:47:14.422 --> 00:47:20.585
It's important to know how the sewers work, how the wastewater flows and all that. If I buy a house, I'll ask you about it.

00:47:21.129 --> 00:47:22.662
Of course. Me too.

00:47:23.860 --> 00:47:26.035
So, what do you tell people who ask you that?

00:47:27.173 --> 00:47:27.954
You don't say "I don't know"?

00:47:28.073 --> 00:47:29.176
No.

00:47:29.295 --> 00:47:30.278
Otherwise, you don't sell.

00:47:30.415 --> 00:47:31.164
Of course.

00:47:31.587 --> 00:47:32.453
So, what do you say?

00:47:38.890 --> 00:47:40.284
You're really mean!

00:47:41.768 --> 00:47:44.762
I'm not mean, I'm just asking the question!

00:47:45.882 --> 00:47:47.461
Normally, it's planned for this area.

00:47:47.988 --> 00:47:48.955
Oh, it's not built yet?

00:47:49.584 --> 00:47:50.789
No, they're building it,

00:47:51.553 --> 00:47:53.607
between here and the other side.

00:47:53.811 --> 00:47:57.139
According to Zouhir Yousfi, the treatment plant is being built here.

00:47:57.597 --> 00:48:00.772
Strangely, as soon as he arrives, he calls his boss.

00:48:02.676 --> 00:48:05.944
These people are driving me crazy. Now they want to see the treatment plant.

00:48:08.497 --> 00:48:15.628
But since it doesn't exist, I brought them here to tell them maybe it was here. They've ruined my day.

00:48:21.754 --> 00:48:22.996
Thanks, goodbye.

00:48:24.796 --> 00:48:26.001
So, what did your boss say?

00:48:31.689 --> 00:48:33.964
Normally, I'm not allowed to make official statements about this.

00:48:35.950 --> 00:48:39.431
Sure, there are some sewer pipes stored here.

00:48:40.263 --> 00:48:44.134
But we're going to find out this isn't where the future treatment plant will be.

00:48:45.492 --> 00:48:46.494
So where is it hiding?

00:48:47.393 --> 00:48:50.076
The region's environmentalists would really like to know.

00:48:50.789 --> 00:48:56.069
A farmer whose land is up in the hills above Saïdia alerted Mohamed, the environmentalist.

00:48:56.969 --> 00:49:02.555
Two months ago, when he came to work his field, Abdou Berkiane made a strange discovery.

00:49:05.005 --> 00:49:08.870
Look, from over there to here, they dug for seven meters.

00:49:09.855 --> 00:49:22.661
The company came without consulting anyone and started digging to install a wastewater pipeline, with no consultation, no permission, no agreement with the owners.

00:49:25.747 --> 00:49:30.348
If a trench was dug through this farmer's wheat field, it's no accident.

00:49:31.129 --> 00:49:34.032
Because a hundred meters away, major work is underway.

00:49:36.680 --> 00:49:42.809
It's here, 10 km from the tourist city, out of sight, that the future treatment plant will be.

00:49:43.539 --> 00:49:47.886
In reality, Saïdia's wastewater will just be held in this big hole.

00:49:50.008 --> 00:49:55.050
A basic filtering and recycling system, and the environmental impact worries the locals.

00:50:00.788 --> 00:50:05.739
It's going to cause problems, sickness, trouble breathing, allergies.

00:50:09.872 --> 00:50:12.419
While waiting for this plant to be up and running,

00:50:13.777 --> 00:50:19.040
a temporary solution was found to handle the waste from the first 3,000 tourists in Saïdia.

00:50:20.042 --> 00:50:24.388
It's in this container that the wastewater is stored before being dumped into the sea.

00:50:29.702 --> 00:50:31.281
A damaged environment,

00:50:31.723 --> 00:50:35.084
a beach resort plagued by delays and shoddy work.

00:50:36.952 --> 00:50:40.449
Saïdia was supposed to be the engine for this neglected region.

00:50:42.147 --> 00:50:45.152
But today, the locals are at least skeptical.

00:50:47.162 --> 00:50:54.609
We're not against tourism. We're for tourism, but the kind that makes us money without losing what we have.

00:50:56.637 --> 00:50:59.006
And what are we leaving for future generations?

00:50:59.431 --> 00:51:03.319
If tomorrow my daughter asks me, "Why did you do this?"

00:51:04.032 --> 00:51:05.237
what will we say?

00:51:05.984 --> 00:51:08.633
Families are getting more and more worried every day.

00:51:09.040 --> 00:51:12.623
They're afraid mass tourism will eventually destroy the whole beach.

00:51:13.217 --> 00:51:16.375
It's about money, it's about the economy, it's business.

00:51:16.816 --> 00:51:20.280
If the tourist site works, they'll build more.

00:51:21.485 --> 00:51:23.922
We hope it doesn't work. Oh yeah!

00:51:26.544 --> 00:51:29.040
It's too soon to tell, but one thing's for sure.

00:51:29.550 --> 00:51:34.049
The king's goal of attracting more and more tourists has been pushed back by several years.

00:51:34.949 --> 00:51:36.935
Of the six planned beach resorts,

00:51:37.461 --> 00:51:41.621
two will probably never see the light of day, and the other four are seriously behind schedule.

00:51:44.117 --> 00:51:49.906
At the start of June, Moroccan families are enjoying the nice weather and their last moments of peace on the beach.

00:51:50.568 --> 00:51:55.441
When Saïdia is running at full speed, it'll be hard for them to find a spot in the sun.

00:51:58.276 --> 00:52:01.468
The rich people take the best parts of the beach.

00:52:02.096 --> 00:52:03.438
If things keep going like this,

00:52:04.202 --> 00:52:06.188
we'll need tickets just to get on the beach.

